Asserting on exact source code strings using `toContain` is highly fragile and can easily break due to minor formatting changes (such as switching between single/double quotes, adding/removing spaces, or trailing semicolons). Using regular expressions with `toMatch` makes these architectural assertions much more resilient to formatting updates.

```suggestion
    expect(context).toMatch(/import\s+\{\s*authenticateRequest\s*\}\s+from\s+['"]\.\.\/\.\.\/middleware\/auth\.js['"]/);
    expect(context).toMatch(/const\s+authResult\s*=\s*await\s+authenticateRequest\(\s*req\s*,\s*\{\s*optional:\s*false\s*\}\s*\)/);
    expect(middleware).toMatch(/const\s+userProfile\s*=\s*await\s+fetchActiveUserProfile\(\s*userId\s*\)/);
